{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es2022",
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["es2023", "dom", "dom.iterable"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true
  },
  // ADR 0009's ratchet: an explicit include list, seeded with modules that pass
  // clean under `strict` on their JSDoc alone. A file added here never leaves —
  // new modules land here on arrival, and existing ones join as their types are
  // written. `npm run typecheck` is a gate, not a report.
  "include": [
    "src/domain/units.js",
    "src/domain/geo.js",
    "src/domain/vertical.js",
    "src/domain/fresnel.js",
    "src/store.js",
    "src/catalog/batteries.js",
    "src/catalog/drones.js",
    "src/catalog/manufacturers.js",
    "src/catalog/payloads.js",
    "src/catalog/scenarios.js",
    "src/catalog/weather.js",
    "src/domain/wind/terrain-forcing.js",
    "src/domain/wind/regime.js",
    "src/domain/mission/mission-schema.js",
    "src/domain/mission/mission-migrations.js",
    "src/domain/mission/mission-reducer.js",
    "src/domain/mission/altitude.js",
    "src/domain/mission/compile.js",
    "src/application/terrain/sample-grid.js",
    "src/application/analysis/analysis-contracts.js",
    "src/application/analysis/constraints.js",
    "src/application/analysis/vertical-flight.js",
    "src/application/analysis/route-checks.js",
    "src/application/analysis/wind-advisory.js",
    "src/application/analysis/analyze.js",
    // ADR 0004's map seam. The contract and the registry import nothing, and the
    // four layers below them import the contract and (for coordinate maths)
    // domain/geo.js, which is already here — so the whole set arrived clean.
    //
    // Two files under presentation/map/ are deliberately *not* here, and both
    // for the same reason rather than for want of types: an include entry pulls
    // its whole import graph into the program, and leaflet-adapter.js reaches
    // vendor/leaflet/leaflet-src.esm.js — 10k lines of unannotated third-party
    // JS that `checkJs` would then check. map-view.js imports the adapter, so it
    // inherits the exclusion. They join if the vendor bundle ever ships types.
    //
    // Wave C's segment-inspector.js is out for the same structural reason and no
    // other: it reads its number formatting off src/render/format.js, which is
    // the display vocabulary the render modules share and is not annotated. It
    // joins when format.js does; duplicating a clock to buy a ratchet entry
    // would be the worse trade.
    "src/presentation/map/map-adapter.js",
    "src/presentation/map/layer-registry.js",
    "src/presentation/map/layers/launch-layer.js",
    "src/presentation/map/layers/footprint-layer.js",
    "src/presentation/map/layers/route-layer.js",
    "src/presentation/map/layers/spots-layer.js",
    // M5's advisory pair arrives clean for the same reason the four layers above
    // did: the layer reaches only the contract and domain/geo.js, and the panel
    // reaches only the layer — it renders its own text rather than borrowing
    // src/render/format.js, which is what keeps it out of segment-inspector.js's
    // position. scene-layers.js draws the same zones from the same exports and
    // still stays out, on deck.gl's account, not theirs.
    "src/presentation/map/layers/advisory-layer.js",
    "src/presentation/map/advisory-panel.js",
    // Wave B's two zero-cost additions. tile-sources.js imports nothing at all;
    // scene-geometry.js reaches only domain/geo.js and route-layer.js, both
    // already here. Their two neighbours under scene3d/ — scene.js and
    // scene-layers.js — stay out for the same reason leaflet-adapter.js does:
    // an include entry pulls the whole import graph in, and theirs is maplibre-gl
    // and deck.gl. Those ship real .d.ts files, but they are devDependencies of a
    // lazily bundled chunk, and pulling ~200 files of engine typings into a
    // browser-ESM typecheck buys nothing this ratchet is for. The pure half is
    // where the arithmetic that can be wrong lives, and it is checked.
    "src/presentation/map/tile-sources.js",
    "src/presentation/map/scene3d/scene-geometry.js",
    "src/domain/terrain/terrain-features.js",
    "src/application/terrain/terrain-contracts.js",
    "src/application/terrain/elevation-cache.js",
    "src/application/terrain/sample-corridor.js",
    "src/infrastructure/elevation/open-meteo-elevation.js",
    // M6 wave A. The XML reader imports nothing at all; adapter-contracts.js
    // reaches only the compiler's typedefs, which are already here.
    "src/infrastructure/export/adapter-contracts.js",
    "src/infrastructure/export/xml.js",
    // M6 waves B/C adapters — entries added ahead of the files (include patterns
    // that match nothing are ignored), so parallel waves never contend for this list.
    "src/infrastructure/export/gpx.js",
    "src/infrastructure/export/kml.js",
    "src/infrastructure/export/qgc-plan.js",
    "src/infrastructure/export/ardupilot-wpl.js",
    "src/infrastructure/export/inav-mission.js",
    // Wave D (ADR 0010 §6): the registry over the five adapters above, all
    // already here — so the router arrived clean on the same account they did.
    "src/infrastructure/export/import-router.js",
    "src/infrastructure/persistence/mission-repository.js",
    "src/infrastructure/persistence/indexeddb-store.js",
    "src/infrastructure/persistence/memory-store.js",
    // The mission bridge takes its seed and its launch-restored callback from the
    // caller rather than importing src/state.js, which is exactly what keeps it
    // checkable here.
    "src/mission-bridge.js",
    // Self-contained (zero imports), so typing its own body was enough — no
    // dependency stood between it and the ratchet.
    "src/domain/physics.js",
    // M7 waves A/B (ADR 0011) — entries added ahead of the files, same as the
    // M6 adapters above: camera.js imports nothing; cameras.js is a catalog.
    // The two scene-* paths are optional extractions (patterns matching nothing
    // are ignored) so the schema wave can split without touching this list.
    "src/domain/camera.js",
    "src/catalog/cameras.js",
    "src/domain/mission/scene-schema.js",
    "src/domain/mission/scene-commands.js",
    // M7 wave D's 2D subject layer — pre-seeded the same way; it reaches only
    // the map contract and domain/geo.js, both above. Its 3D siblings stay out
    // on deck.gl's account, like the rest of scene3d/.
    "src/presentation/map/layers/subject-layer.js",
    // src/state.js is fully typed too (M3), but an include entry pulls its whole
    // import graph into the program, and that graph still isn't clean: it reaches
    // src/data.js, src/registry.js, src/flightlog.js, src/packinstances.js,
    // src/windprofile.js, src/terrain.js, src/rf.js, src/calibrate.js and
    // src/catalog/classes.js (214 implicit-any/null errors between them, none of
    // it state.js's own). It joins once those are typed, not before.
    //
    // src/analysis-host.js and src/mission-commands.js were named here as
    // state.js's companions, waiting on the same M3 typing — that's only half
    // true. Once state.js itself is clean, both still reach further unannotated
    // modules of their own: analysis-host.js through src/domain/route.js,
    // src/terrain.js, src/render/dashboard.js and src/render/terrain.js;
    // mission-commands.js through src/weather.js, src/presentation/map/map-view.js
    // and src/render/controls.js. Typing state.js does not clear either path.
    //
    // src/render/missions.js was grouped with them on the same assumption, but it
    // doesn't import src/state.js or src/domain/physics.js at all — its only
    // imports are this mission-bridge.js entry (already clean) and
    // src/render/dom.js (11 errors of its own). It has never been blocked by the
    // physics/state work; it is waiting on dom.js alone.
    //
    // Wave D's own top-level module. Unlayered like mission-bridge.js, and clean
    // for the same reason: every dependency it reaches — compile.js,
    // adapter-contracts.js, import-router.js — is already on this list, and it
    // takes its own missionDocument/importMission/groundAt by injection rather
    // than importing src/mission-bridge.js or src/analysis-host.js directly.
    "src/interop.js",
  ]
}
